'''CVE-2026-109618''' and '''CVE-2026-109619''', collectively referred to as '''PHP2Shell''', are two [[Vulnerability (computer security)|vulnerabilities]] in [[PHP]] versions 5.4 – 8.5 that allow [[Arbitrary code execution|remote code execution]] (RCE). Their existence was publicly announced on 20 October 2026.<ref name="oct20">{{Cite web |date=2026-10-20 |title=PHP: News Archive - 20 Oct 2026 |url=https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-20-1 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.php.net |language=en}}</ref> An emergency [[Patch (computing)|patch]] and details about the vulnerabilities were released on the following day.<ref name="oct21">{{Cite web |date=2026-10-21 |title=PHP: News Archive - 21 Oct 2026 |url=https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-21-5|access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.php.net |language=en}}</ref>

==Discovery==
PHP versions as early as 5.4 are affected by these vulnerabilities, and while this type of bug had been identified as an area of concern decades before, no action was taken at the time as it was not thought that an exploit would be feasible.<ref name="Khandelwal2026-2">{{cite news |last1=Khandelwal |first1=Swati |title=PHP Issues Emergency Patches for Two Critical PHP2Shell RCE Flaws as Exploit Attempts Emerge |url=https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/php-issues-emergency-patches-for-two.html |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=The Hacker News |date=21 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref>{{Unreliable source |reason="The Hacker News" uses AI for article images, so it likely also uses AI for article text. |date=October 2026}}<ref name="McMillan2026" details="{{subref|q=The class of bugs had been hiding in PHP’s source code for years. A security researcher had flagged it as a problem area decades earlier, but the flaw had been considered too difficult to exploit and was largely forgotten, Duong said.}}" /> A PHP core developer discovered the out-of-memory error behind one of the vulnerabilities during an audit in 2024, but the development team could not reach consensus of how to fix the issue, and since a specific exploit had not yet been discovered, the work to resolve it was not completed.<ref name="Constantin2026" />

The two vulnerabilities were independently discovered by the California-based<ref>{{cite news |last1=Volz |first1=Dustin |title=A Hacking Tool Built With A.I. Can Breach Phones Without a Click |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/08/us/politics/calif-ai-worm-wechat-hack.html |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=The New York Times |date=8 September 2026}}</ref> security firm Calif as part of the [[OpenAI]] "Patch the Planet" project in August 2026, and by the Israeli-American cloud security company [[Wiz, Inc.]]<ref name="Constantin2026">{{cite news |last1=Constantin |first1=Ana Maria |title=PHP knew about one of the flaws in 2024 but could not agree a fix |url=https://thenextweb.com/news/php-foundation-structural-failure-2024-flaw-leak |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=The Next Web |date=21 October 2026}}</ref><ref name="McMillan2026" details="{{subref|q=Ryan Austin, a researcher at Calif, uncovered the flaws with help from GPT-5.6 Sol, one of OpenAI’s most advanced models, during an audit of PHP’s source code in August. ... Researchers from Google’s Wiz cybersecurity team soon reached out with new exploits, which they had discovered independent of Calif. }}">{{cite news |last1=McMillan |first1=Robert |title=Exclusive {{!}} AI Just Found a Bug That Puts up to 70% of the World’s Websites at Risk |url=https://www.wsj.com/tech/cybersecurity/ai-just-found-a-bug-that-puts-up-to-70-of-the-worlds-websites-at-risk-fd0902ea |url-access=subscription |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=Wall Street Journal |date=21 October 2026}}</ref> 

The vulnerability was rated 9.2 (critical) out of 10 under [[Common Vulnerability Scoring System|CVSS]] v4.0 by PHP developers,<ref>{{Cite web |title=php.announce: [ANNOUNCE] Upcoming Critical PHP Security Release |url=https://news-web.php.net/php.announce/509 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=news-web.php.net}}</ref> while security firm watchTowr rated it a full 10.0.<ref name="Website-3">{{Cite web |last=Abrams |first=Lawrence |title=PHP warns admins of two critical flaws, emergency patches coming tomorrow |url=https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/php-warns-admins-of-two-critical-flaws-emergency-patches-coming-tomorrow/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=BleepingComputer |language=en-us}}</ref>

==Patches==
The maintainers of PHP released patched versions of PHP 8.2, 8.3, 8.4, and 8.5 (8.2.35, 8.3.36, 8.4.27, and 8.5.12).<ref name="oct21" /> Source code patches were released for version 7.4, 8.0, and 8.1, but no official updated binaries for those versions were released as they are considered [[Software release life cycle#End-of-life|end of life]].<ref name="oct21" /> Temporary mitigations were also published, but the maintainers said there were no complete mitigations and that updating was the only safe choice.<ref name="oct21" /> Both vulnerabilities were published through [[GitHub]] security advisories on 21 October at 13:34 UTC,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w|title=RCE via OOM corruption|date=2026-10-21|access-date=2026-10-21}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://github.com/php/php-src/security/advisories/GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp|title=RCE via stale global state|date=2026-10-21|access-date=2026-10-21}}</ref> 26 minutes before the announced release time, with the PHP website updated shortly thereafter.{{citation needed|date=October 2026}}

The PHP maintainers announced the update early and before release, after news of the vulnerabilities leaked on October 20th on the [[Hacker News]] forums, in a post on [[X (social network)|X]] by cybersecurity company watchTowr labs, and in a proof-of-concept video mistakenly released by Calif.<ref name="Khandelwal2026-1">{{cite news |last1=Khandelwal |first1=Swati |title=PHP2Shell: Two Unpatched Critical PHP RCE Flaws (CVSS 9.2) Trigger Emergency Security Alert |url=https://thehackernews.com/2026/10/php2shell-two-unpatched-critical-php.html |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=The Hacker News |date=21 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref>{{Unreliable source |reason="The Hacker News" uses AI for article images, so it likely also uses AI for article text. |date=October 2026}}<ref name="McMillan2026" details="{{subref|q=News of the patch leaked out Tuesday, in a post to Hacker News, just under two days before the patch release.}}"/><ref name="Constantin2026" /> The early notice on PHP.net encouraged users to prepare and update as soon as the patch was released.<ref name="Khandelwal2026-1" />{{Unreliable source |reason="The Hacker News" uses AI for article images, so it likely also uses AI for article text. |date=October 2026}}<ref name="oct21" /> One security firm, Sansec, expected "mass attacks to start within 15 minutes of the release".<ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Goodin |first=Dan |date=2026-10-20 |title=PHP warns it plans to release 2 emergency patches imminently |url=https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/php-maintainers-tell-web-devs-to-prepare-themselves-for-critical-security-patches/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=Ars Technica |language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261021121524/https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/php-maintainers-tell-web-devs-to-prepare-themselves-for-critical-security-patches/|archive-date=2026-10-21|url-status=live}}</ref> [[Proof of concept|PoCs]], write-ups, and partial mitigations started circulating on the Internet before the end of the embargo after development platform Chainguard released patched versions of the software early, allowing attackers to determine what code had been changed.<ref name="Website-3" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Only for the curious... |url=https://jackalsyn.com/posts/php-up-2026.html |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=jackalsyn.com}}</ref><ref name="Constantin2026" />

[[Cloudflare]] reported signs of early exploitation prior to public [[Patch (computing)|patch]] release, and deployed temporary [[web application firewall]] (WAF) rules as an attempted stopgap mitigation against the [[Vulnerability (computer security)|vulnerability]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Knecht |first1=Dane |title=Dane Knecht 🦭 (@dok2001) on X |url=https://x.com/dok2001/status/2112811965735202970 |website=X (formerly Twitter) |date=21 October 2026}}</ref><ref name="Gatlan2026">{{Cite web |last=Gatlan |first=Sergiu |date=2026-10-21 |title=PHP ships emergency patches for critical PHP2Shell flaws |url=https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/php-ships-emergency-patches-for-critical-php2shell-flaws/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=BleepingComputer |language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-10-21 |title=Hold on to your butts: Web admins braced for emergency PHP updates to plug leaked exploits |url=https://www.pcgamer.com/software/security/we-expect-mass-attacks-to-start-within-15-minutes-of-the-release-web-admins-braced-for-emergency-php-updates-to-plug-leaked-high-severity-holes/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |work=PC Gamer |language=en}}</ref> Security firm watchTowr said it had observed exploitation in the wild by 21 October through its honeypot network,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-20 |title=PHP RCE FAQ: PHP2Shell CVE-2026-109618 & CVE-2026-109619 |url=https://watchtowr.com/intelligence/php-cve-2026-109618-faq/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=watchTowr |language=en}}</ref> while the US [[Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency]] (CISA) said it had not observed exploitation as of the same day.<ref name="Collier2026" />

== Impact ==
Approximately 69% of websites use PHP, including those that use [[content management system]]s such as [[Drupal]], [[Joomla]], and [[WordPress]], so the potential impacts are widespread.<ref name="Collier2026">{{cite news |last1=Collier |first1=Kevin |title=Critical vulnerabilities in commonly used web code spark rush to update systems |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/security/critical-vulnerabilities-php-update-systems-rcna604751 |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=NBC News |date=21 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Gerkin |first1=Tom |title=Websites taken down worldwide as critical PHP cyber-security update issued |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmly0lg3w5x2o |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=BBC News |date=21 October 2026}}</ref> Following advice from [[Computer emergency response team|CERT]]-EU and the British [[National Cyber Security Centre (United Kingdom)|National Cyber Security Centre]], many organizations across [[Europe]] took their websites offline as a precaution while the patches were pending, including the [[Netherlands|Dutch]] government portal Overheid.nl, the [[Denmark|Danish]] national school communication platform, all [[Finland|Finnish]] government public-facing web services, and all websites of the [[Ministry of the Interior (France)|French Interior Ministry]].<ref name="Satter2026">{{Cite news |last=Satter |first=Raphael |last2=Marchandon |first2=Leo |last3=Ghosh |first3=Kanjyik |last4=Pardesi |first4=Rajveer |date=2026-10-21 |title=Government websites go dark after AI-discovered flaw sparks security scramble |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/eu-agency-warns-websites-may-need-be-taken-offline-php-prepares-patches-2026-10-21/ |access-date=2026-10-21 |work=Reuters |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-10-21 |title=Websites taken down as critical PHP cyber-security update issued |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cmly0lg3w5x2o |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=www.bbc.com |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Van Dessel |first=Josse  |date=October 21, 2026 |title=Major websites go offline as critical PHP vulnerability triggers security fears |url=https://www.brusselstimes.com/belgium/2371464/major-websites-go-offline-as-critical-php-vulnerability-triggers-security-fears |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=The Brussels Times |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release|url=https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/news/critical-security-issue-affecting-php|publisher=National Cyber Security Centre|date=October 21, 2026|title=Critical security issue affecting PHP}}</ref> The US [[National Security Agency]] recommended that websites go offline if unable to update immediately.<ref name="Kan2026">{{cite news |last1=Kan |first1=Michael |title=This Flaw Is So Serious Websites Should Consider Going Offline, NSA Says |url=https://www.pcmag.com/news/php-flaw-is-so-serious-websites-should-consider-going-offline-nsa-says |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=PCMAG |date=21 October 2026 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="NSA">{{cite web|url=https://www.nsa.gov/Press-Room/Press-Releases-Statements/Press-Release-View/Article/4630238/nsa-advisory-php-updates/ |title=NSA Advisory: PHP Updates |author=<!-- not stated--> |publisher=[[National Security Agency|NSA]] Press Room |date=October 21, 2026 |access-date=October 21, 2026 |quote=Effective immediately, all organizations using PHP for their systems are advised to be prepared for an update addressing two critical vulnerabilities.}}</ref> The Japanese National Cybersecurity Office advised PHP users to immediately update their systems.<ref name="ITmedia-21oct2026" /> The [[Wikimedia Foundation]] shut down many of their supporting services, and forum sites [[4chan]] and [[Something Awful]] went offline.<ref name="Collier2026" /><ref>{{cite news |title=Impact of PHP vulnerabilities on Wikimedia tools and services |url=https://diff.wikimedia.org/2026/10/21/impact-of-php-vulnerabilities-on-wikimedia-tools-and-services/ |access-date=21 October 2026 |work=Diff |publisher=Wikimedia Foundation |date=21 October 2026}}</ref><ref name="Kan2026" /> Parts of the [[Japan]]ese internet also went offline, including games such as ''[[Umamusume: Pretty Derby]],''<ref>{{Cite web |title=News｜ウマ娘 プリティーダービー 公式ポータルサイト｜Cygames |url=https://umamusume.jp/news/detail?id=3523 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=umamusume.jp |language=ja}}</ref> ''[[Shadowverse: Worlds Beyond]]'', [[Final Fantasy XIV]],<ref>{{Cite web |publisher=SQUARE ENIX Inc. |author=<!-- not stated --> |title=Notice Regarding Temporary Suspension of New Logins Across All Worlds (Oct. 21) |url=https://na.finalfantasyxiv.com/lodestone/news/detail/682d63eac6bb6c380a0b1b16bbeb0efd45c13ad5 |access-date=2026-10-21 |website=FINAL FANTASY XIV, The Lodestone |language=en-us}}</ref> and many online commerce sites.<ref name="ITmedia-21oct2026">{{cite news|url=https://www.itmedia.co.jp/news/article/2610/21/2000002472/|date=October 21, 2026|title=著名ITサービス相次いで緊急メンテへ　コドモン、シャドバ、BASE、さくらのレンタルサーバなど　「PHP」脆弱性に起因か|publisher=ITmedia|access-date=October 21, 2026 |language=Japanese}}</ref>

